From ggrothendieck at gmail.com  Sun Jul  5 00:01:27 2009
From: ggrothendieck at gmail.com (Gabor Grothendieck)
Date: Sat, 04 Jul 2009 20:01:27 -0400
Subject: Re: RJDBC and quoting of column names
In-Reply-To: <E5018519-2985-49AB-AA30-F70016334FED@r-project.org>
References: <971536df0907031737q6eb9a633id125c34d7254d44d@mail.gmail.com> <E5018519-2985-49AB-AA30-F70016334FED@r-project.org>
Message-ID: <971536df0907041701t5b178522sb6f1a53093c7e8b9@mail.gmail.com>

But this isn't SQL written by the user; its SQL written by dbWriteTable and
the generated CREATE statement could quote the column names.

Also note that most commonly used SQL databases do allow quoting of
identifiers in which case they are treated in a case sensitive manner.

What is the use of the quote.identifier= argument in JDBC if they are not
quoted?

On Sat, Jul 4, 2009 at 2:01 PM, Simon
Urbanek<simon.urbanek at r-project.org> wrote:
>
> On Jul 3, 2009, at 8:37 PM, Gabor Grothendieck wrote:
>
>> Consider the following code using the H2 database where we are using the
>> built in BOD data frame.  Note that the column names come out in upper case.
>
> SQL is by definition case-agnostic, so that is to be expected... (Some DBMS
> break that rule at their own risk ...).
>
>
>>  I have inserted a print statement into dbWriteTable and you can see the
>> sql statement that dbWriteTable generates.  Shouldn't that sql statement
>> have double quotes around the column names?
>
> The logic in RJDBC is that if an identifier is of the form
> <letter><letter/number/_> then it won't be quoted. At least in standard SQL
> it should make no difference. (However, your post made me think about this
> -- and in the cases of keywords the current logic may not be sufficient
> ...).
>
> Cheers,
> Simon
>
>
>
>>  I did specify identifier.quote= in
>> the JDBC call.
>>
>>> library(RJDBC)
>>> # '"' is single quote, double quote, single quote
>>> drv <- JDBC("org.h2.Driver", "C:\\Program Files\\H2\\bin\\h2.jar",
>>> identifier.quote = '"')
>>> con <- dbConnect(drv, "jdbc:h2:mem:", "sa", "")
>>> dbWriteTable(con, "b", BOD)
>>
>> [1] "CREATE TABLE b (Time DOUBLE PRECISION,demand DOUBLE PRECISION)"
>> [1] TRUE
>>>
>>> dbGetQuery(con, "select * from b")
>>
>>  TIME DEMAND
>> 1    1    8.3
>> 2    2   10.3
>> 3    3   19.0
>> 4    4   16.0
