References: <971536df0907031737q6eb9a633id125c34d7254d44d@mail.gmail.com> <E5018519-2985-49AB-AA30-F70016334FED@r-project.org> <971536df0907041701t5b178522sb6f1a53093c7e8b9@mail.gmail.com> <75E2B918-9BC5-4DD3-8AB3-C4B2B5AA72B5@r-project.org>
Message-ID: <971536df0907050634t52b2d8f8w4ca66eb808b8aa1@mail.gmail.com>

Can you clarify.  My understanding is that in this case the
column names require quoting and I did specify quote.identifier
but didn't get any quotes with the result that I got upper case
column names using H2/JDBC.

The same dbWriteTable statement with RSQLite gives mixed
case column names.

There seems to be no way to get mixed case column names
with dbWriteTable here.

On Sun, Jul 5, 2009 at 9:15 AM, Simon
Urbanek<simon.urbanek at r-project.org> wrote:
>
> On Jul 4, 2009, at 8:01 PM, Gabor Grothendieck wrote:
>
>> But this isn't SQL written by the user; its SQL written by dbWriteTable
>> and
>> the generated CREATE statement could quote the column names.
>>
>> Also note that most commonly used SQL databases do allow quoting of
>> identifiers in which case they are treated in a case sensitive manner.
>>
>> What is the use of the quote.identifier= argument in JDBC if they are not
>> quoted?
>>
>
> So it can be used on identifiers that require quoting  (see my previous
> e-mail) ...
>
> Cheers,
> S
>
>
>> On Sat, Jul 4, 2009 at 2:01 PM, Simon
>> Urbanek<simon.urbanek at r-project.org> wrote:
>>>
>>> On Jul 3, 2009, at 8:37 PM, Gabor Grothendieck wrote:
>>>
>>>> Consider the following code using the H2 database where we are using the
>>>> built in BOD data frame.  Note that the column names come out in upper
>>>> case.
>>>
>>> SQL is by definition case-agnostic, so that is to be expected... (Some
>>> DBMS
>>> break that rule at their own risk ...).
>>>
>>>
>>>>  I have inserted a print statement into dbWriteTable and you can see the
>>>> sql statement that dbWriteTable generates.  Shouldn't that sql statement
>>>> have double quotes around the column names?
>>>
>>> The logic in RJDBC is that if an identifier is of the form
>>> <letter><letter/number/_> then it won't be quoted. At least in standard
>>> SQL
>>> it should make no difference. (However, your post made me think about
>>> this
>>> -- and in the cases of keywords the current logic may not be sufficient
>>> ...).
>>>
>>> Cheers,
>>> Simon
>>>
>>>
>>>
>>>>  I did specify identifier.quote= in
>>>> the JDBC call.
>>>>
>>>>> library(RJDBC)
>>>>> # '"' is single quote, double quote, single quote
