This puts me in a good position to implement "call" and perhaps "docall" 
methods so that we can do things like:

r.call( "rnorm", 100, 10 )

or

r.call( new REXPSymbol("rnorm"), new Object[]{ 100, 10 } )

if we stick to non java 1.5 syntax ...


"docall" would make sense when we are calling a function, __but__ at the 
moment we can only get references to functions:

BeanShell> r.parseAndEval( "rnorm", r.globalEnv, false )
org.rosuda.REngine.REXPReference@dc0435{eng=org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine@98350a,h=171585844}
BeanShell> r.parseAndEval( "rnorm", r.globalEnv, true ) == null
true

I have to check what makes sense in a "REXPFunction" and will make a 
proposal in another thread.


for the call method, I think these variants are appropriate;

- for unnamed arguments

REXP call( REXPSymbol fn, Object[] arguments )

REXP call( String fn, Object[] arguments ) as a shortcut to :
	call( new REXPSymbol(fn), arguments )

- for (potentially) named arguments:

REXP call( REXPSymbol fn, REXPList arguments )
REXP call( String fn, REXPList arguments )

or maybe a new set of constructors to the REXPLanguage class is what is 
needed here.

Anyway, as Simon pointed out in another thread, we would need a method 
that would wrap an Object into a REXP so that we can call :

call( "rnorm", 10 )
instead of
call( "rnorm", new REXPInteger(10))

I think it would make sense to have this a method of the REngine class 
to deal with most cases (REXP, int, int[], ...) and overload it in 
JRIEngine so that we can push references to java objects using the 
createRJavaRef method of the Rengine..


