Hi,

Bilel MASMOUDI wrote:
> Hi,
>
> In order to let a java program using R transportable,
Could you explain more what you mean by "R transpotable"?

> So, I removed the %R_HOME%/bin and the %JRI_HOME% from the windows path
> and I wrote this code to modify the path dynamically :
>
>         String path = System.getProperty("java.class.path");
>         path += ";C:\\R\\R-2.9.0\\bin";
>         path += ";C:\\R\\R-2.9.0\\library\\rJava\\jri";
>         System.out.println(path);
>         System.setProperty("java.class.path", path);
>
>         rengine = ConnectionToR.simpleConnect();
>
If I am guessing correctly what you are trying to do above, here are 
some hints:

1) You can never change the class path once you have started the Java 
virtual machine. Or to put it differently: You would need to install a 
custom class loader to do that, which is not that easy.

2) For JRI to work on windows - at least from my experience - you need 
to have the following: Valid R_HOME env. variable, R_HOME/bin in the 
path, "java.library.path" pointing to JRI dir (so like c:\program 
file\R\R-2.9.1\library\rJava\jri).
You should try to set these before you start your Java VM which uses 
JRI, if you can. There are ways to do this differently, but it will be 
much harder.

3) If you could explain what you are trying to achieve I could probably 
answer better.

Bernd

